# 0002-setup-budget.patch — `/api/setup` gets its own rate-limit budget.
#
# Files: src/common/config.rs        (RateLimitConfig fields + defaults + env
#                                     parsing + config tests)
#        src/common/config_file.rs   (TOML -> env bridge table, KNOWN)
#        src/main.rs                 (separate limiter + startup log line)
#        src/interfaces/middleware/rate_limit.rs  (repoint ONE test — see
#                                     "LAYERING" below)
#        docs/config/env.md          (the two new variables)
#
# POLICY FLAG: this is a policy change, not a correctness fix, and it is the one
# patch in this series the user can drop without losing a fix. Dropping it means:
# `/api/setup` keeps sharing the register budget (5/3600 keyed by client IP), so
# register traffic can still lock the first-run wizard out and a mistyped setup
# attempt still spends registration budget.
#
# LAYERING (read before building a differential arm)
#   * GENERATED AGAINST 0001 = sha256
#       29dd53b9a5af216e0598dcdbbe0a204213eccced06117f4c108fd8e85bcb4098
#     Use the pair from the same row of docs/re/patches/README.md. Pairing this
#     file with an older 0001 (e.g. 751ca5a0...) or an older 0002 with this 0001
#     fails at src/interfaces/middleware/rate_limit.rs (~line 526): the hunk is a
#     CONTEXT diff against 0001's test module, and the guard test added in
#     that revision shifts it. `git apply --check` the pair before building an arm:
#       git apply 0001-limiter-window.patch &&
#       git apply --check 0002-setup-budget.patch
#   * Every file except rate_limit.rs is clean at HEAD, and this patch applies to
#     it directly.
#   * The rate_limit.rs hunk is a CONTEXT DIFF AGAINST 0001's test module: it
#     rewrites `two_routes_share_one_budget` (which 0001 leaves asserting the
#     shared budget — true of the tree at HEAD) into
#     `setup_and_register_have_separate_budgets`. That test lives in 0001's
#     patch, so this one cannot apply to a clean tree on its own; the arm to
#     build is HEAD+0001+0002. The alternative — putting the repoint in 0001 —
#     was rejected because it would make the HEAD+0001 arm carry a test that
#     asserts wiring 0001 does not change.
#
# WHAT IT CHANGES (behaviour)
#   1. `RateLimitConfig` gains `setup_max_requests` (5) and `setup_window_secs`
#      (3600), read from `OXICLOUD_RATE_LIMIT_SETUP_MAX` /
#      `OXICLOUD_RATE_LIMIT_SETUP_WINDOW_SECS` next to the register/login/
#      refresh pairs, and registered in the `KNOWN` TOML->env bridge so
#      `[auth.rate_limit] setup_max = 2` works from a config file too.
#   2. `main.rs` builds a dedicated `setup_limiter` from those values and layers
#      it on `setup_route()` instead of `register_limiter`. The startup line now
#      prints `setup: {max}/{window} s` as well.
#   3. Defaults are deliberately the SAME numbers register had (5/3600), so an
#      untouched deployment allows exactly as much setup traffic as before; what
#      changes is who owns the counter.
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CHANGE
#   * The middleware function on the setup route is still `rate_limit_register`:
#     the limiter INSTANCE decides the budget, and adding a `rate_limit_setup`
#     wrapper would have to live in rate_limit.rs, which is 0001's file (two
#     patches editing the same function = a conflict, and 0001 must stand alone).
#     Consequence: the limiter's `tracing::warn!` on a setup 429 says
#     `endpoint = "register"`. Cosmetic; fix it in a patch that owns
#     rate_limit.rs if it bothers an operator.
#   * `/api/auth/login` and the OPAQUE login route keep sharing `login_limiter`:
#     that sharing is intentional (one password-guessing budget across both
#     password surfaces).
#   * No CORS/reverse-proxy limits, no per-account cap, no persistence: the
#     counter is still per-process and in-memory, so a multi-replica deployment
#     still multiplies the effective ceiling by the replica count.
#
# TESTS THAT PIN IT
#   * common::config::setup_rate_limit_budget_tests::default_is_five_per_hour —
#     the documented defaults.
#   * common::config::setup_rate_limit_budget_tests::
#     setup_env_vars_do_not_move_the_register_budget — the split property: the
#     new vars move only the setup fields, and register/login keep their own
#     defaults. Goes through `AppConfig::from_env()`, so it fails if the parsing
#     is broken (a `RateLimitConfig::default()` assertion would not).
#   * common::config_file::tests::known_table_covers_every_env_var — an existing
#     test that scans `src/` for `env::var("OXICLOUD_...")`; it fails until the
#     two new names are added to `KNOWN`, which is why that edit is part of this
#     patch rather than an optional extra.
#   * rate_limit::tests::setup_and_register_have_separate_budgets — both
#     directions: spend register's budget (on handler-level 400s), `/setup`
#     still has its own two slots; and with fresh instances, spend setup's
#     budget and register's slots are untouched.
#
# RED/GREEN
#   Not a unit-RED patch: the limiter test constructs the two instances it
#   asserts about, which v0.10.0 can do as well, so it is a guard against a
#   future re-merge, not evidence of the fix. The observable evidence is at the
#   HTTP layer against a real server, two arms:
#     A (v0.10.0 / HEAD):  5× POST /api/auth/register (429s included) then
#                          POST /api/setup → 429 (setup locked out by register).
#     B (this patch):      same burst, then POST /api/setup → 201/400 (setup
#                          untouched), and a setup burst leaves register's
#                          budget intact.
